>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'MC3R-deficient mice are mildly obese without eating more or burning less. The receptor is not a second appetite switch.',
      'In a fast, the receptor is required to release fatty acids from fat, to load triglyceride into the liver, to drive refeeding, and to activate the stress axis. The fat cells themselves can still respond to a direct stimulus.',
      'It is also required for food-anticipatory activity: waking and shifting metabolism before a meal that always comes at the same hour.',
      'In people, loss of MC3R delays puberty, shortens linear growth, and lowers lean mass and IGF-1. The same system that senses a fast is the one that decides a child is fed enough to mature.',
      'No approved drug is aimed at this physiology. Inflammatory and obesity programmes that touch MC3R are doing something else.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> is the receptor people mean when they say melanocortins
    control appetite. <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a> sits in the same hypothalamus, reads
    the same peptides, and does not do that job. Take it away and a mouse does not become
    ravenous. It becomes a little fatter, a little less lean, and strangely bad at noticing
    that it has not eaten.<Cite n={1} /> This page is that physiology. The receptor card
    itself — structure, variants, the common double haplotype — stays on the{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Obesity without hunger</h2>
  <p>
    The MC3R-knockout mouse was confusing for years because it broke the only story the
    field had. Melanocortin failure was supposed to mean more food and more fat, the way
    losing MC4R does. These mice had more fat and less lean mass, and they were not
    hyperphagic and not hypometabolic in any simple sense.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={4} /> The
    fat accumulated because the animal handled a change in food badly, not because it
    asked for more of it. γ-MSH is a relatively selective natural agonist here, and AgRP
    antagonises the receptor, but the phenotype is not “AgRP won.” It is “the report that
    nutrition changed never went out.”<Cite n={4} />
  </p>

  <h2>The fast that does not register</h2>
  <p>
    A fast is a coordinated instruction. Fat tissue releases fatty acids, the liver takes
    some of them up as triglyceride, the animal eats hard when food returns, and the
    adrenal stress axis wakes up to hold blood glucose. In wild-type mice a sixteen-hour
    fast raises circulating fatty acids. In MC3R-deficient mice it does not. Liver
    triglyceride does not rise either. Put the same fat tissue in a dish and stimulate it
    directly, and it releases fatty acids perfectly well — so the lipase machinery is
    intact. What is missing is the order to use it.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The same animals also fail the other half of the fast. They do not show the normal
    refeeding drive, and fasting does not regulate their adipostatic signals or fully
    activate the hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal axis. Their corticosterone nadir sits
    high, and the fasting-induced rise is blunted. The authors noted that the package —
    fat up, lean and bone down, corticosterone awry — resembles Cushing physiology, which
    is a warning about interpretation, not a claim that these mice have Cushing’s.<Cite
      n={1}
    /> The point that survives is narrower and solid: MC3R is how nutritional deprivation
    is announced to hypothalamus, adrenal, fat, and liver at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Knowing a meal is coming</h2>
  <p>
    Quantity is only half of nutrition. Timing is the other half, and it belongs to this
    receptor more than to any other in the family. Restrict mice to a single daily meal
    and they learn it. Activity and waking rise in the hours before the food, and clocks
    in peripheral organs shift to meet it. Mice without MC3R lose that food-anticipatory
    behaviour and no longer entrain those clocks to feeding time.<Cite n={2} /> They can
    still eat. They cannot get ready.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a different statement from “MC3R controls circadian rhythm.” Light still sets
    the master clock. What fails is the feeding-dependent clock: the one that should move
    when dinner moves. Sutton and colleagues’ result is why MC3R is described as the
    receptor for nutritional timing, and why a page about appetite that only mentions
    MC4R is missing a limb.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>The same report, read as permission to grow</h2>
  <p>
    The human evidence arrived later and made the mouse result legible. People with
    loss-of-function MC3R variants, including one person with two broken copies, had later
    puberty, less linear growth, less lean mass, and lower IGF-1. Mice lacking the
    receptor were late to mature, and their reproductive cycle stopped answering normally
    to nutritional state.<Cite n={3} /> A childhood body asks one question of its energy
    system before it spends years on a growth spurt: is there enough? MC4R answers how
    much to acquire. MC3R answers whether the answer is yes.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The common partial variants, and the rare complete ones, are catalogued on the{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">receptor page</a> and under <a href="/genetics">genetics</a>.
    They are not the same phenotype. Partial loss leans toward a fatter child. Complete
    loss leans toward a child who grows and matures late.
  </p>

  <h2>What drug programmes are not doing</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing in the clinic is trying to restore the fasting response or the timing of
    puberty. <a href="/resomelagon">Resomelagon</a> is a biased MC1R/MC3R agonist aimed at
    inflammation. Dual MC3R/MC4R agonists sit in the <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> as an
    obesity idea, still preclinical, folded onto the receptor page because they are not yet
    their own story. Both are uses of the protein. Neither is a treatment for the
    physiology on this page, and it would be a mistake to read a mouse fasting defect as a
    reason to take either.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    MC3R is the melanocortin receptor for a change of state. A fast, a meal on a schedule,
    a childhood with or without enough food: those are its inputs, and fat-versus-lean,
    anticipation, and the clock of puberty are its outputs. For the ligand that prefers
    it, see <a href="/gamma-msh">γ-MSH</a>; for the appetite receptor it is forever
    compared with, <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a>.
  </p>

